            {
              "id": "glog-4e92e458-6bd4-4d48-b18b-9241e0214558",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the function `ctime` is used, which can lead to potential security issues.\n\nThe `ctime` function converts a time_t value into a string, representing the local time. The problem with `ctime` is that it returns a pointer to a static buffer, making it non-reentrant and non-thread-safe. If the function is called from multiple threads, it can cause data races and undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-reentrant and non-thread-safe functions like `ctime`. Instead, use alternatives that are reentrant and thread-safe. In this case, you can use `ctime_r`, which is a reentrant version of `ctime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `ctime` function with `ctime_r`. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\nchar buffer[26];\ntime_t rawtime = time(nullptr);\n\nif (ctime_r(&rawtime, buffer) != nullptr) {\n    jitsafe_header_ctime = buffer;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<ctime>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
